a simple java spring app that plays withmysql  databases
Modern Java.
THe plan is to run this app and tune the database usage up and down while displaying the differnece on a stats page. I eventually want to try out different database setups and experiment with clustering. This is a POC. Maybe later there will be a db admin portal so bear that in mind but dont impliment.
1 html page showing database statistics. This is about testing the database and not writing better sql queries.
I want to write random strings to a database along with some numbers 3 coloums total. Words, Number, ID.
Each string is from a language and one language corrosponds to one database.
I want a stats page showing the current database performance. I want faily detaild statistics but I still want to just run a simple java application.
I want to configure the number of readers and writers
I want these readers and writers to use 1 database connection each.
The code should not repeat.
I want to be able to configure the number of connections avalible for each reader and writer
Each Writer should be configurable if it writes to one database or many
Each reader should be configurable if it reads to one database or many
No HIkari nothing fancy. if it cant be done without then do the bad version.
We dont care about tests.
Code must be concise and follow clean code practices.

---

running it

  docker compose -f ../servers/mysql.docker_compose.yml up -d
  ./gradlew bootRun

The page is on http://localhost:8080. The same numbers are on /api/stats as json.
Java 25 and Spring Boot 4.1. The gradle wrapper is here. Nothing else needs installing.

terms

client. One reader or one writer. Both are headless.
fleet. Every client in one run.
tick. One scheduled run of one client. Each client ticks on its own interval.
scope. Which databases one client uses. It is all of them or one of them.
graph. The small line beside a number. It is one line per number and it holds the last 60 polls.

tuning it

Every setting is in src/main/resources/application.yml. Any setting is also an argument.

  ./gradlew bootRun --args='--app.writers.count=8 --app.readers.count=4'

  app.writers.count        how many writers. app.readers.count does the same for readers
  app.writers.connections  connections for one writer. one each by default
  app.writers.scope        all or one
  app.writers.interval     how often one writer ticks
  app.writers.clients[0]   index and scope and language and connections for one writer only
  app.stats.interval       how often the page samples the server
  app.stats.refresh        how often the page reloads itself

A writer inserts one row per database per tick. A reader counts the rows and reads the newest row.
The page is the only thing served.

how it works

One database per language. strings_english and strings_german and strings_french and strings_russian
and strings_japanese. Each holds one table of id and words and number. All of them are created on
startup. No url selects a database. Every statement names its own.

There is no pool library. Each client owns a fixed set of connections. Its pool hands out one at a
time. A count of one allows one statement at a time. A client waiting for its own connection is inside
its avg ms. Raising the count shows that cost falling.

The page reads SHOW GLOBAL STATUS and SHOW GLOBAL VARIABLES and the processlist and information_schema.
It marks the processlist rows that belong to this app. It counts every language's rows exactly. The
information_schema estimate can then be compared against the exact count.

graphs

Every graph is drawn in the browser. The server keeps no history and it never has. Each poll the page
carries one number per graph. The browser appends it to what it already has and draws the line itself.
That history sits in sessionStorage. A reload between two polls is ignored so no number is counted
twice. A new tab starts with nothing and closing the tab throws the history away.

The scale runs from zero to the highest number in the window. A flat line is a steady number and not a
rounding artefact. Hover a graph for its lowest and highest and newest number. Only a number that moves
is graphed. A constant such as max connections is text alone.
